# NO RULES HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Read this before adding one.
#
# `@sveltejs/adapter-netlify` copies this file verbatim into the publish
# directory (`build-netlify/_redirects`) when it sits next to `netlify.toml`.
# `adapter-node` has never heard of it, so the Node target is untouched either
# way. It must NOT live in `static/` — the adapter throws if it finds it there,
# because `static/` is served under `paths.base` and a redirect file has to sit
# at the site root.
#
# THIS FILE USED TO CARRY A BARE-ROOT FRONT DOOR:
#
#     /    /showcase/credit-risk/sveltekit/    308
#
# the same 308 `server.mjs` performs locally, so that opening the Netlify site
# URL itself lands on the mount instead of a 404. IT NEVER FIRED, and it cannot:
#
#   Netlify's request chain evaluates SERVERLESS FUNCTIONS BEFORE REDIRECTS
#   (https://docs.netlify.com/platform/request-chain/). This adapter emits ONE
#   function registered on `path: ["/*"]` — see `netlify.toml` for why that is
#   the only routing that preserves `kit.paths.base` — so `/` matches the
#   function first and the redirect step is never reached. `preferStatic: true`
#   is the sole escape hatch, and it yields only to a STATIC FILE, never to a
#   rule in this file. Forcing the rule (`308!`) changes nothing either: `force`
#   orders a redirect against static files, not against functions.
#
# MEASURED, not reasoned about. With the rule present, `netlify dev` serving the
# real build (`--dir build-netlify --functions .netlify/functions-internal`)
# answers `/` with SvelteKit's own plain-text `Not found`, 404, and never logs
# the redirect. Drop the identical file into a static-only site with no function
# and the rule 308s correctly — so the file parses and the rule is well formed;
# it is simply shadowed.
#
# WHAT `/` DOES INSTEAD. The function receives it, SvelteKit sees a path outside
# `kit.paths.base`, and returns `Not found` with status 404 before any hook runs.
# That is cosmetic: every real request arrives through the docs-site proxy
# already carrying `/showcase/credit-risk/sveltekit/`, and every page, asset and
# in-app 404 under that prefix is served correctly. It only bites someone who
# opens the raw Netlify URL to eyeball a deploy.
#
# IF A FRONT DOOR IS WANTED, `preferStatic: true` is the seam, and a static file
# is the only thing it yields to — confirmed by dropping an `index.html` at the
# publish root and watching it win over the function. That means a post-build
# step writing `build-netlify/index.html` (a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` plus
# a real link), because the adapter rimrafs the publish directory on every
# build. It would be an HTML hop rather than a 308, and it would put the first
# and only `.html` file into a publish directory that is otherwise pure assets.
# Not worth it for a URL nothing routes through, so it is written down here
# rather than built.
